[{"order":1,"type":"heading","text":"File I/O: Reading and Writing Files in Core Java"},{"order":2,"type":"paragraph","text":"File Input/Output (I/O) is a fundamental aspect of programming, allowing your Java applications to interact with files on the file system. This tutorial will guide you through the process of reading from and writing to files in Java, covering various techniques and best practices."},{"order":3,"type":"heading","text":"Understanding Streams"},{"order":4,"type":"paragraph","text":"In Java, I/O operations are performed using streams. A stream represents a sequence of data. There are two main types of streams:"},{"order":5,"type":"bullet_point","text":"**Input Stream:** Used for reading data from a source (e.g., a file)."},{"order":6,"type":"bullet_point","text":"**Output Stream:** Used for writing data to a destination (e.g., a file)."},{"order":7,"type":"paragraph","text":"Java provides several classes for working with streams, categorized into byte streams and character streams. Byte streams handle raw bytes, while character streams handle characters, providing encoding/decoding capabilities."},{"order":8,"type":"heading","text":"Reading Files"},{"order":9,"type":"subheading","text":"Using FileReader and BufferedReader (Character Streams)"},{"order":10,"type":"paragraph","text":"The `FileReader` class is used to read character files. It's often wrapped with `BufferedReader` for efficient reading, as `BufferedReader` provides buffering."},{"order":11,"type":"code","text":"import java.io.BufferedReader;
import java.io.FileReader;
import java.io.IOException;

public class ReadFileExample {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        String fileName = "input.txt";

        try (BufferedReader br = new BufferedReader(new FileReader(fileName))) {
            String line;
            while ((line = br.readLine()) != null) {
                System.out.println(line);
            }
        } catch (IOException e) {
            System.err.println("Error reading file: " + e.getMessage());
        }
    }
}"},{"order":12,"type":"paragraph","text":"Explanation:"},{"order":13,"type":"bullet_point","text":"We create a `BufferedReader` wrapped around a `FileReader`, specifying the file name."},{"order":14,"type":"bullet_point","text":"The `try-with-resources` statement ensures that the `BufferedReader` is closed automatically, even if exceptions occur."},{"order":15,"type":"bullet_point","text":"The `readLine()` method reads a line of text from the file until a newline character is encountered. It returns `null` when the end of the file is reached."},{"order":16,"type":"bullet_point","text":"We handle `IOException` to catch any errors that might occur during file reading."},{"order":17,"type":"subheading","text":"Using FileInputStream and Scanner (Byte Streams)"},{"order":18,"type":"paragraph","text":"The `FileInputStream` class is used to read raw bytes from a file.  It can be combined with the `Scanner` class for parsing data."},{"order":19,"type":"code","text":"import java.io.FileInputStream;
import java.io.IOException;
import java.util.Scanner;

public class ReadFileScannerExample {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        String fileName = "input.txt";

        try (Scanner scanner = new Scanner(new FileInputStream(fileName))) {
            while (scanner.hasNextLine()) {
                String line = scanner.nextLine();
                System.out.println(line);
            }
        } catch (IOException e) {
            System.err.println("Error reading file: " + e.getMessage());
        }
    }
}"},{"order":20,"type":"paragraph","text":"Explanation:"},{"order":21,"type":"bullet_point","text":"We create a `Scanner` object that reads from a `FileInputStream`."},{"order":22,"type":"bullet_point","text":"The `hasNextLine()` method checks if there is another line to read."},{"order":23,"type":"bullet_point","text":"The `nextLine()` method reads the next line from the file."},{"order":24,"type":"subheading","text":"Reading All Lines at Once (Java 8+)"},{"order":25,"type":"paragraph","text":"Java 8 introduced the `Files` class, which provides convenient methods for reading all lines of a file into a `List<String>`."},{"order":26,"type":"code","text":"import java.io.IOException;
import java.nio.file.Files;
import java.nio.file.Paths;
import java.util.List;

public class ReadAllLinesExample {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        String fileName = "input.txt";

        try {
            List<String> lines = Files.readAllLines(Paths.get(fileName));
            lines.forEach(System.out::println);
        } catch (IOException e) {
            System.err.println("Error reading file: " + e.getMessage());
        }
    }
}"},{"order":27,"type":"paragraph","text":"Explanation:"},{"order":28,"type":"bullet_point","text":"We use `Files.readAllLines()` to read all lines from the file into a `List<String>`."},{"order":29,"type":"bullet_point","text":"We then iterate through the list and print each line."},{"order":30,"type":"heading","text":"Writing Files"},{"order":31,"type":"subheading","text":"Using FileWriter and BufferedWriter (Character Streams)"},{"order":32,"type":"paragraph","text":"The `FileWriter` class is used to write character files. It's often wrapped with `BufferedWriter` for efficient writing."},{"order":33,"type":"code","text":"import java.io.BufferedWriter;
import java.io.FileWriter;
import java.io.IOException;

public class WriteFileExample {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        String fileName = "output.txt";

        try (BufferedWriter bw = new BufferedWriter(new FileWriter(fileName))) {
            bw.write("This is the first line.\n");
            bw.write("This is the second line.\n");
            bw.write("This is the third line.\n");
        } catch (IOException e) {
            System.err.println("Error writing file: " + e.getMessage());
        }
    }
}"},{"order":34,"type":"paragraph","text":"Explanation:"},{"order":35,"type":"bullet_point","text":"We create a `BufferedWriter` wrapped around a `FileWriter`, specifying the file name."},{"order":36,"type":"bullet_point","text":"The `try-with-resources` statement ensures that the `BufferedWriter` is closed automatically."},{"order":37,"type":"bullet_point","text":"The `write()` method writes a string to the file. We use `\n` to add a newline character."},{"order":38,"type":"subheading","text":"Using FileOutputStream and PrintWriter (Byte Streams)"},{"order":39,"type":"paragraph","text":"The `FileOutputStream` class is used to write raw bytes to a file. It can be combined with `PrintWriter` for convenient formatting and writing of data."},{"order":40,"type":"code","text":"import java.io.FileOutputStream;
import java.io.IOException;
import java.io.PrintWriter;

public class WriteFilePrintWriterExample {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        String fileName = "output.txt";

        try (PrintWriter pw = new PrintWriter(new FileOutputStream(fileName))) {
            pw.println("This is the first line.");
            pw.println("This is the second line.");
            pw.println("This is the third line.");
        } catch (IOException e) {
            System.err.println("Error writing file: " + e.getMessage());
        }
    }
}"},{"order":41,"type":"paragraph","text":"Explanation:"},{"order":42,"type":"bullet_point","text":"We create a `PrintWriter` object that writes to a `FileOutputStream`."},{"order":43,"type":"bullet_point","text":"The `println()` method writes a string to the file, followed by a newline character."},{"order":44,"type":"subheading","text":"Writing All Lines at Once (Java 8+)"},{"order":45,"type":"paragraph","text":"Java 8 also provides a convenient method for writing a `List<String>` to a file using the `Files` class."},{"order":46,"type":"code","text":"import java.io.IOException;
import java.nio.file.Files;
import java.nio.file.Paths;
import java.util.Arrays;
import java.util.List;

public class WriteAllLinesExample {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        String fileName = "output.txt";
        List<String> lines = Arrays.asList("This is the first line.", "This is the second line.", "This is the third line.");

        try {
            Files.write(Paths.get(fileName), lines);
        } catch (IOException e) {
            System.err.println("Error writing file: " + e.getMessage());
        }
    }
}"},{"order":47,"type":"paragraph","text":"Explanation:"},{"order":48,"type":"bullet_point","text":"We create a `List<String>` containing the lines to write."},{"order":49,"type":"bullet_point","text":"We use `Files.write()` to write the lines to the file."},{"order":50,"type":"heading","text":"Appending to Files"},{"order":51,"type":"paragraph","text":"To append data to an existing file, you need to use the `FileWriter` or `FileOutputStream` constructors that accept an `append` flag."},{"order":52,"type":"code","text":"import java.io.BufferedWriter;
import java.io.FileWriter;
import java.io.IOException;

public class AppendToFileExample {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        String fileName = "output.txt";

        try (BufferedWriter bw = new BufferedWriter(new FileWriter(fileName, true))) {
            bw.write("This line is appended.\n");
        } catch (IOException e) {
            System.err.println("Error appending to file: " + e.getMessage());
        }
    }
}"},{"order":53,"type":"paragraph","text":"Explanation:"},{"order":54,"type":"bullet_point","text":"We pass `true` as the second argument to the `FileWriter` constructor to enable append mode."},{"order":55,"type":"heading","text":"Handling Exceptions"},{"order":56,"type":"paragraph","text":"File I/O operations can throw `IOException`s. It's crucial to handle these exceptions properly to prevent your application from crashing. The `try-catch` block is used for exception handling.  The `try-with-resources` statement is highly recommended to ensure that resources (like streams) are closed properly, even if exceptions occur. This avoids resource leaks."},{"order":57,"type":"heading","text":"Best Practices"},{"order":58,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Always close streams in a `finally` block or use `try-with-resources` to prevent resource leaks."},{"order":59,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Use buffered streams (`BufferedReader`, `BufferedWriter`) for efficient I/O."},{"order":60,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Handle `IOException`s appropriately."},{"order":61,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Consider using the `Files` class (Java 8+) for simpler file operations."},{"order":62,"type":"heading","text":"Conclusion"},{"order":63,"type":"paragraph","text":"This tutorial covered the basics of file I/O in Java, including reading from and writing to files using various stream classes. By following the best practices and handling exceptions properly, you can create robust and efficient file I/O operations in your Java applications."}]
